            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

Overall, your house does not have a lot of excess air leakage, however there are some key areas where air leakage occurs. I did note some air leakage around the patio door, as well as some around the utilidor penetration and crawlspace exterior access door.



I also noted some air leakage around your attic hatch. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $130&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;3.5 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 studs with 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation, as well as 1.5 inches of XPS insulation. I have modelled doubling the amount of rigid XPS insulation to 3.0 inches, increasing the R value of your walls by at least R7.5. 



I recommend that wall insulation upgrades be done when you are also undertaking a siding renovation. Your siding is currently in good shape and your walls are already well insulated, so I would place a lower priority on this upgrade.  



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding R7.5 of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $370&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;9.8 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$1,125 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



</mainWalls>
                <ceilings />
                <windows>

As all of your windows are older double pane ones that do not appear to have a Low E coating, I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading all of them with ENERGY STARÂ® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. The kitchen window to the right of your patio door is already a triple paned window, so I have not included this in my upgrade recommendations.



In general, your windows are in good physical condition and I did not notice any mold or physical damage. I also did not note significant air leakage around the frames or seals. As discussed while I was on site, I would not necessarily make this upgrade a high priority unless there are other reasons to replace your windows (i.e. physical damage). 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing all your windows with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STARÂ® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $605&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;15.9 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</windows>
                <doors>

As you mentioned the possibility of replacing your patio door, I have modelled replacing it with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified patio door rated for climate zone 3. 



Your patio door is already in good shape and you would not see any gains from an upgrade. I suggest replacing your weatherstripping as necessary and possibly some air sealing around the frame. Hinged patio doors such as the one in your home are able to seal better than sliding doors, so should this door need to be replaced at any point I recommend staying with a hinged door. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your patio door with ENERGY STARÂ® qualified patio door rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $0.0&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;0.0 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new patio doors from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</doors>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your current natural gas fired boiler was originally rated at 87.3% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency condensing natural gas boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. If you choose a high-efficiency condensing natural gas boiler, the current chimney can be eliminated altogether.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing natural gas boiler with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $330&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;8.9 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



Because you have a combi system where your hot water is supplied by your boiler, upgrading your boiler will also increase the efficiency of your hot water tank. You could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $130&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;3.6GJ&lt;/b&gt;, in addition to the savings on your space heating costs.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>

Harnessing solar energy in the NWT may seem unlikely. The short and dark days of winter provide little or no usable energy, depending on the latitude in the NWT. However, the long hours of sunlight in spring and summer can provide usable heat and electricity for up to eight months of the year.  As prices continue to drop, solar PV panels are becoming competitive with the cost of diesel generation. 



The back of your house has a southwest exposure with no shading, making this an excellent option for your home. I have modelled a standard 3 kW system, but more capacity may be a possibility. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing a 3 kW solar photovoltaic system, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $865&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;10.4 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$5,000 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program. 



&lt;b&gt; GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program rebate amount differs according to the purchase cost of the system. You would be eligible for 1/3 of the purchase cost, up to a maximum of $5,000 per year. Applicants are strongly encouraged to contact the AEA for pre-approval prior to installation. Projects will be funded on a first come, first serve basis until the program is fully subscribed. Projects must be installed and operational to receive the rebate. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/alternative-energy-technologies-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</Renewables>
                <AdditionalComments>

&lt;b&gt;ECM Circulation Pumps:&lt;/b&gt;



Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to ÂlearnÂ the systemÂs usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house you can qualify for &lt;b&gt;$175 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



